WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:30.398
Astronomers traced a brief dimming in aging stars to a shrinking entropy difference inside their convective zones. This is Fascinating Frontiers, Episode 255. Euclid's gravitational lens catalog, a reanalysis of galaxy UHZ1, and three cores of massive star formation, then the cosmic deep dive. Low mass stars like the Sun, according to Fizz.org, exhaust the hydrogen in their cores and climb the red giant branch, growing brighter as they cool and expand. A short drop in

00:00:30.398 --> 00:00:35.578
luminosity cuts across that climb and shows up in color magnitude diagrams of stellar clusters

00:00:35.578 --> 00:00:39.498
as the red giant branch or RGB luminosity bump.

00:00:40.118 --> 00:00:44.338
A color magnitude diagram is a plot of how bright the stars are against their color.

00:00:45.018 --> 00:00:50.218
Inside these red giants, an inert helium core shrinks as a hydrogen burning shell moves outward

00:00:50.618 --> 00:00:55.578
and the convective envelope, the outer layer of rising and sinking gas, grows deeper.

00:00:55.578 --> 00:01:00.058
However, once the base of that envelope hits layers changed by earlier nuclear burning,

00:01:00.518 --> 00:01:05.038
a mixing called Dreadjup stirs the material through and alters the envelope's entropy

00:01:05.038 --> 00:01:05.658
profile.

00:01:06.238 --> 00:01:11.058
Models put the bump at higher luminosities than clusters actually show, and the entropy

00:01:11.058 --> 00:01:13.998
mechanism is now being tested to close that timing gap.

00:01:15.013 --> 00:01:18.513
ESA's Euclid telescope has put out its first major data release.

00:01:19.093 --> 00:01:24.773
The release covers 1.7 million gigabytes and charts almost 5% of the sky, and more

00:01:24.773 --> 00:01:30.293
More than 2,000 scientists identified over 900 million galaxies and 60 million quasars.

00:01:30.813 --> 00:01:35.473
A quasar is a galaxy whose center outshines the rest as matter falls into a black hole.

00:01:36.113 --> 00:01:41.073
A strong gravitational lens forms when a nearer galaxy bends light from a farther galaxy

00:01:41.073 --> 00:01:42.873
into arcs or Einstein rings.

00:01:43.353 --> 00:01:47.353
And fewer than 1,000 confirmed strong lenses were known before Euclid.

00:01:47.813 --> 00:01:52.333
While the new catalog adds 15,000 candidates, the largest collection assembled.

00:01:52.333 --> 00:01:58.173
Those lenses work as natural magnifying glasses and show how invisible dark matter is arranged

00:01:58.173 --> 00:01:59.373
in the nearer galaxies.

00:02:00.113 --> 00:02:04.853
Citizen scientists and machine learning pipelines helped refine the candidate list before expert

00:02:04.853 --> 00:02:10.173
checks with follow-up observations, and that same uniform data set will also tighten constraints

00:02:10.173 --> 00:02:15.093
on dark energy by testing how the lenses' parameters fit current cosmological models.

00:02:16.069 --> 00:02:21.669
Galaxy UHZ1 had earlier been claimed to host a heavily dust-obscured black hole formed

00:02:21.669 --> 00:02:25.369
by direct collapse and already massive when the universe was young.

00:02:25.949 --> 00:02:30.109
Direct collapse means a black hole forming from gas without a star dying first.

00:02:31.289 --> 00:02:35.909
Reprocessed x-ray data combined with fresh infrared observations show no detectable hidden

00:02:35.909 --> 00:02:37.049
black hole activity.

00:02:37.629 --> 00:02:42.929
The reanalysis in the astrophysical journal interprets the galaxy as a low mass, metal

00:02:42.929 --> 00:02:46.069
poor, star forming system rather than a quasar host.

00:02:46.069 --> 00:02:55.988
According to Fizz.org, Alma observations targeted the massive dense clump catalogued as G 40.282-0.219.

00:02:56.348 --> 00:03:03.468
That clump is one parent structure, a single reservoir of gas, and inside it the array resolved three dense cores.

00:03:04.128 --> 00:03:11.188
Astronomers mapped those three cores and found that they sit at successive evolutionary stages, not three copies of the same moment.

00:03:11.188 --> 00:03:16.288
Each core is at a different stage of massive star formation, and all three still belong

00:03:16.288 --> 00:03:17.768
to that same parent clump.

00:03:18.348 --> 00:03:22.928
The data reveal physical differences from one core to the next, differences that line up

00:03:22.928 --> 00:03:24.228
with those successive stages.

00:03:24.748 --> 00:03:28.308
The same observations reveal chemical differences among the cores as well.

00:03:28.928 --> 00:03:32.828
Those physical and chemical properties are what trace the progression of massive star

00:03:32.828 --> 00:03:41.028
formation as it unfolds inside one gas clump, which is why G40.282-0.219 holds three

00:03:41.028 --> 00:03:42.768
chapters of the process at once.

00:03:43.639 --> 00:03:49.159
Comet 220 p. mcnaught, according to space.com, suddenly brightened this summer.

00:03:49.739 --> 00:03:51.199
The jump was not a small flare.

00:03:51.759 --> 00:03:56.859
The comet surged in brightness by thousands of times, escaping gases tore the comet apart

00:03:56.859 --> 00:03:58.919
and drove that huge brightness surge.

00:03:59.499 --> 00:04:04.319
Gases that had been trapped inside broke out so rapidly they fractured the nucleus, the

00:04:04.319 --> 00:04:05.439
comet's solid body.

00:04:05.979 --> 00:04:10.039
Once the nucleus fractured, fresh ice that had been locked away were laid bare.

00:04:10.039 --> 00:04:16.299
air. Those newly exposed ices produce the dramatic outburstspace.com reported. The whole chain,

00:04:16.659 --> 00:04:21.439
the fractured nucleus, the exposed ices, and the thousands of times rise in brightness,

00:04:22.139 --> 00:04:27.159
traces back to that rapid release of trapped gases this summer. That brings us to the cosmic

00:04:27.159 --> 00:04:33.450
deep dive. Why aging stars briefly dim? A low mass star like the Sun can spend billions

00:04:33.450 --> 00:04:39.110
of years on the main sequence, fusing hydrogen in its core at a steady pace. That is the long

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:43.550
middle of the star's life, and nothing in this story requires anything to strike it from

00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:48.970
the outside. The middle ends when the core hydrogen runs out. The core contracts, the

00:04:48.970 --> 00:04:54.010
outer layers expand. The star leaves that long plateau and starts up the red giant branch.

00:04:54.590 --> 00:05:00.770
On the climb it grows larger, it grows cooler, and it grows more luminous. Cluster observations

00:05:00.770 --> 00:05:05.750
have followed that steady brightening for a long time, star by star, in systems whose

00:05:05.750 --> 00:05:10.830
members form together. Then the brightening is interrupted. Observations of star clusters

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:16.170
show a short-lived dip in the middle of the rise. That dip is the RGB luminosity bump.

00:05:16.730 --> 00:05:21.810
The same feature that appears in color magnitude diagrams as a distinct wad of stars off the

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:27.790
otherwise smooth climb. The cause sits inside the envelope. As the star ascends, the convective

00:05:27.790 --> 00:05:33.330
envelope, the outer zone of rising and sinking gas, keeps deepening. An inert helium core

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:38.790
is shrinking at the same time, and a hydrogen burning shell is advancing outward, so the envelope

00:05:38.790 --> 00:05:44.010
has more and more room to reach down. Eventually the base of that envelope arrives at layers

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:48.830
whose composition was altered by earlier nuclear burning. Material that had been sitting

00:05:48.830 --> 00:05:53.990
deeper is mixed outward. That mixing is the dredge up, and it changes the entropy profile

00:05:53.990 --> 00:05:59.170
of the envelope. After the mixed material is spread through, the specific entropy difference

00:05:59.170 --> 00:06:04.190
across the base of the convective zone shrinks. There is a boundary layer there, and the entropy

00:06:04.190 --> 00:06:09.850
jump across it is the quantity that matters. Once that entropy jump falls below a critical threshold,

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:16.110
the star's luminosity decreases for a time. The dip is temporary. After it, the climb resumes,

00:06:16.530 --> 00:06:20.990
and the star continues up the red giant branch as before. The catches in the models,

00:06:21.450 --> 00:06:24.910
they place the bump at higher luminosities than real clusters display.

00:06:24.910 --> 00:06:29.470
The observed feature arrives earlier in the climb than the calculations say it should,

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:33.710
and that timing mismatch is the reason the entropy picture is being examined.

00:06:34.250 --> 00:06:36.950
The mechanism is being tested as an internal switch,

00:06:37.450 --> 00:06:40.090
a change in the entropy difference across the boundary layer,

00:06:40.490 --> 00:06:43.110
that might put the bump where the clusters actually show it,

00:06:43.470 --> 00:06:45.430
rather than where the models have been putting it.

00:06:45.850 --> 00:06:47.670
The process is entirely internal.

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:49.790
No external trigger is required.

00:06:49.790 --> 00:06:55.290
What remains unknown is the precise entropy value at which the boundary layer becomes unstable.

00:06:55.850 --> 00:06:59.070
How that value changes with stellar mass is open as well.

00:06:59.670 --> 00:07:01.650
And so is how it changes with medallicity.

00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:05.010
A path to those answers runs through the clusters themselves.

00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:11.370
Measure how deep the bump is and when it happens in large samples of clusters whose ages are already known.

00:07:11.970 --> 00:07:13.970
Those measurements would pin down the threshold.

00:07:14.490 --> 00:07:17.650
They would also pull the models into closer agreement with the data.

00:07:17.650 --> 00:07:23.933
Ordinary processes inside stars and galaxies keep turning into effects we can actually observe,

00:07:24.173 --> 00:07:29.453
and this dip is one of them, a shrinking entropy difference, a brief fade, then the climb

00:07:29.453 --> 00:07:29.793
again.

